A hot, aggressive dryer can still leave hair feeling rough even if it has premium-sounding claims.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Is wattage the most important thing in a hair dryer?</summary><p class="faq-answer">No. Wattage only tells you part of the story. Airflow design, heat control, weight, button placement, and filter maintenance matter more in daily ownership.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>What hair dryer features are actually worth paying for?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Controlled heat settings, useful airflow, a real cool shot, comfortable weight, a removable filter, and attachments you will actually use. Extra modes and bundled accessories are only worth it if they match your routine.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>Why do hair dryers stop working well over time?</summary><p class="faq-answer">The common problems are clogged rear filters, overheating, weak motors, loose attachments, cracked nozzles, worn buttons, and cords that get twisted or stressed during daily bathroom use.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>What should curly or coily hair buyers check before buying?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Do not just buy the strongest dryer. Check the diffuser, low-heat control, airflow gentleness, and whether the listing includes the attachment setup you actually need. The wrong Shark or diffuser configuration can make a good dryer feel wrong for textured hair.</p></details></div></div></div></div></section><section class="num-section" id="related-decisions"><div class="num-wrap"><div class="num-split"><div class="num-left"><span class="eyebrow">Related decisions</span><h2>Related decisions</h2></div><div><p class="related-intro">Useful next decisions if you are comparing related hair dryer or household purchases.</p><div class="related-grid"><div class="related-card"><h3>Best Hair Straightener for Most People</h3><p>For buyers building a simple, reliable hair-styling setup without o